# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
u"""Which visual area a cell is in, and the Shih lab layers.                             2026-09-28

Søren, after the Shih lab preprint (How, Danskin, Pedigo, Memke, Stamenkovic, Kleinfeld & Shih,
"Axons organize into micro-tracts around the cortical vasculature", bioRxiv 2026,
https://doi.org/10.64898/2026.09.26.754479 — which cites his own 2023 vessel counts):

    "I would like to add a layer to uJump filtering with the areameshes and also the possibility
     to add the vascular skeletonizations."
    "Yes, restricting within the areas but we also need to draw the areas to show where they are.
     so both."
    "I don't just want the V1 column, we already have that. I want the areameshes where they
     discern what major area of the visual cortex we are in."

THE MESHES ARE WALLS, NOT AREAS. gs://allen-minnie-phase3/areameshes is a
neuroglancer_legacy_mesh — four segments, 2,038 to 2,982 vertices each, no segment_properties and
no names. None of them is a solid area. Each is a RIBBON: a polyline along the pia (y 241-344 um)
paired vertex-for-vertex with one along the white matter (y 792-952 um), stitched into 2n-2
triangles. Each is the WALL between areas, standing from pia to white matter and leaning ~40 um
sideways on the way down.

Read as four closed areas they make one degenerate loop of near-zero signed area, which is what
sent the first reading wrong. Drawn, the answer is obvious: the four walls meet at ONE point near
(x 1135, z 623) um at the pia and radiate outwards in four branches. That is the "junction of
VISp and three higher visual areas" the MICrONS papers describe
(https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-08790-w). Each mesh carries exactly the two branches
that bound its own area, so adjacent meshes lie on top of one another along the branch they share.

    branch N  is shared by meshes 0 and 2        sector between N  and NE is mesh 2
    branch NE is shared by meshes 2 and 1        sector between NE and SE is mesh 1
    branch SE is shared by meshes 1 and 3        sector between SE and S  is mesh 3
    branch S  is shared by meshes 3 and 0        sector between S  and N  is mesh 0

So a cell's area is: build the four branches at THAT CELL'S OWN DEPTH (the walls lean, so depth
matters), find the nearest branch, and read which side of it the cell is on. Not a bounding box,
not a fitted plane — the same shape the Allen Institute drew.

WHICH AREA IS WHICH, AND HOW THAT IS KNOWN. The source carries no names, so none of this is
read off the file. Four independent things agree:

  1. THE ALLEN V1 COLUMN. 1,899 hand-classified cells that are by construction inside V1, all of
     which µJump already holds (v1columndata). Every one lands in mesh 0's sector and none in the
     other three. Mesh 0 is also the largest by far and lies on the medial side.
  2. THE TOPOLOGY. In the MICrONS figure Søren sent (Nature 2025, Fig. 1a), VISal is the only one
     of the four that does NOT touch VISp — it sits between the two branches that run out to the
     lateral edge. Mesh 1 is the only sector here that does not touch mesh 0.
  3. WHERE THE JUNCTION SITS. In that figure the four areas meet just above the subvolume 35/65
     boundary at section 14,816. The junction computed from the meshes is at z = 623 µm, i.e.
     section 15,575 — just above it.
  4. WHY ONE AREA IS NEARLY EMPTY. VISlm lies mostly BELOW that boundary, in subvolume 35, which
     this nucleus list does not cover. Mesh 2's sector holds 1,089 nuclei against 21,270 and
     32,242 for the other two, and its two branches run out to z = 218 and z = 460 µm, both below
     the minnie65 floor of 593 µm.

    area 0  V1 (VISp)      89,517 nuclei    the medial side; holds the whole Allen V1 column
    area 3  RL (VISrl)     32,242           the large upper band, across the V1 border
    area 1  AL (VISal)     21,270           the lateral wedge; the one that never touches V1
    area 2  LM (VISlm)      1,089           mostly in subvolume 35, off the end of this list —
                                            which is why the Shih lab state shows 0, 1, 3

WHY THE LABELS ARE BAKED IN. 144,118 cells x ~550 wall segments is not a thing to do inside a
filter loop. The labels are computed here, once, and packed two bits per cell (48 KB of base64).
The geometry travels too (8.8 KB), because a reported cell or a pasted coordinate has no index
into the master array and still has to get an answer; areaAtVox() is that path, and the check
asserts the two agree cell for cell.

THE MESHES COULD NOT BE FETCHED FROM HERE. storage.googleapis.com is refused by the org egress
policy — http 000 from the container and from the device shell, and the gateway logs
connect_rejected for it. They were read through Søren's own browser, which is not behind that
policy, then simplified with Douglas-Peucker at 1 um on the paired 6-vector (1,136 vertices left
of 6,336) and rounded to whole um.

THE SKELETONS. Ten hand-labelled vascular zones from the paper's own Neuroglancer state, plus
skelFinal_v7_branch_order, which Søren picked for the capillary bed ("I agree with the
skelFinal_Branch_Order. That is very relevant.") — it carries eight per-vertex properties, among
them BranchOrder_ACT 1-12 and BranchOrder_CVT 1-8, the paper's Figure 1J/K colouring, and its
shader travels verbatim so the sliders work as they do in the authors' own link. BBs is the tenth
zone; the paper's Methods list ten hand-labelled categories and names nine of them in the Figure 1
legend with exactly the colours this state uses, so BBs is the tenth, pial (P), by elimination —
the label says so rather than inventing a name for it.

These are viewer layers: Neuroglancer fetches gs:// itself, so nothing here has to reach a host
this container cannot.

Check: visualareacheck.js (21 red before, green after).
Run: python3 src/which_visual_area_a_cell_is_in.py
"""
